## 4. Methodology and limitations

### How the scan was run

Hosts and services were discovered with Nmap using the command shown in the report header. {{ ctx.scan_method }}

Each open port on each host that was up was then assessed against recon-report's built-in rules: {{ ctx.service_rule_count }} service exposure rules (cleartext protocols, remote administration, databases and management APIs) and {{ ctx.version_rule_count }} version rules (outdated, end-of-life or known-vulnerable releases). No exploitation, authentication attempts or vulnerability probing were performed.

### Confidence levels

{% for level, note in confidence_notes.items() %}
- **{{ level.value|capitalize }}:** {{ note }}.
{% endfor %}

### Limitations

- Version findings are based only on the version reported in the service banner. {{ ctx.backport_note }}
- Version rule thresholds were last reviewed against official vendor lifecycle and advisory pages on {{ ctx.rules_last_reviewed }}. Releases and end-of-life dates after that are not reflected.
- The scan shows only what was reachable from the scanning host at the time of the scan. Filtered ports, ports and protocols that were not scanned, and changes made after the scan are not covered.
- Findings indicate exposure and likely risk, not confirmed vulnerabilities. The absence of findings does not mean a host is secure.

